# PDC-OS — Stage 1: Kernel Architecture Upgrade

**Target release:** PDC-OS 0.2.0  
**Objective:** Restructure and strengthen the existing kernel so future development does not require major architectural rewrites.

This stage prioritizes architecture, ownership, interfaces, reliability, and maintainability over adding new user-facing features.


Please check [] items when your done. 
example:
not done: []

done: [x]

## 1. Establish the architecture boundaries
- [x] Separate architecture-specific code into `arch/x86_64/`.
- [x] Establish the initial Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL).
- [x] Separate generic kernel logic from hardware operations.
- [x] Define subsystem responsibilities and dependencies.
- [x] Establish consistent initialization and shutdown interfaces.

## 2. Formalize memory management
- [x] Introduce distinct physical and virtual address types.
- [x] Document the virtual address-space layout.
- [x] Define kernel and userspace memory regions.
- [x] Establish page-table and physical-frame ownership.
- [x] Implement reliable address-space cleanup.
- [x] Establish TLB invalidation interfaces.

## 3. Establish the process and thread model
- [x] Separate process metadata from scheduler threads.
- [x] Define process and thread lifecycle states.
- [x] Establish thread-owned kernel stacks.
- [x] Design userspace stack allocation.
- [x] Define process resource ownership and cleanup.
- [x] Prepare structures for independent address spaces.

## 4. Improve execution and interrupt architecture
- [x] Separate generic context management from x86-64 switching.
- [x] Formalize interrupt-frame structures.
- [x] Establish TSS and IST infrastructure.
- [x] Introduce per-CPU data abstractions.
- [x] Define safe interrupt and scheduling boundaries.

## 5. Strengthen synchronization
- [x] Audit existing locks and IRQ-safe locking.
- [x] Establish consistent locking conventions.
- [x] Define blocking and wakeup contracts.
- [x] Identify lock-order dependencies.
- [x] Test contention and interrupt interactions.

## 6. Standardize driver architecture
- [x] Establish device registration interfaces.
- [x] Separate hardware discovery from driver implementations.
- [x] Define driver initialization and removal lifecycles.
- [x] Standardize hardware resource ownership.
- [x] Establish interrupt registration contracts.

## 7. Formalize kernel interfaces
- [x] Define subsystem-facing APIs.
- [x] Standardize kernel error handling.
- [x] Document syscall boundaries and existing limitations.
- [x] Separate architecture-specific interfaces from generic services.
- [x] Establish versioning rules for future ABI development.

## 8. Strengthen kernel security foundations
- [x] Enable NXE where supported.
- [x] Enable CR0.WP.
- [x] Validate memory mappings and permissions.
- [x] Audit pointer validation and arithmetic overflow.
- [x] Identify unsafe Rust boundaries.
- [x] Document current privilege-isolation limitations.

## 9. Build a kernel verification suite
- [x] Test repeated allocation and deallocation.
- [x] Test heap and frame allocator exhaustion.
- [x] Test page-table creation and destruction.
- [x] Test task creation and cleanup.
- [x] Test lock contention and interrupt safety.
- [x] Test malformed boot information.
- [x] Add automated QEMU regression tests.

## 10. Complete the architectural migration
- [x] Migrate existing code incrementally into the new structure.
- [x] Preserve existing boot and shell functionality.
- [x] Update architecture and subsystem documentation.
- [x] Verify reproducible builds.
- [x] Audit unresolved architectural decisions.
